* text=auto eol=lf
# A case's own input files are bytes, not text: what a case hands a program.
# The rule above rewrites CR LF to LF when a text file is committed, so Rush
# 02's crlf.dict, whose line endings ARE its case, reached every clone with
# LF only -- the case tested nothing and ex00_oracle_fixtures failed there,
# while the checkout that wrote it was green. Only a fixtures/ folder one
# level below a tests/ folder is exempt: an exercise's or a unit's
# (tests/exNN/fixtures/, tests/<unit>/fixtures/), or that of any other folder
# there. A file whose CRs are its case goes in its exercise's or unit's, and
# a CR in a clue, a script or an expected output beside it is an editor's,
# which the rule above strips. A folder below one of those
# (tests/exNN/<name>/fixtures/) is not exempt.
# //tools:conventions refuses a file the rule above would rewrite (a CR LF,
# and no lone CR, which makes git keep the file as it is) that no -text or
# binary rule here covers, a CR in a harness text file (a clues*.tsv, a
# script, a BUILD or .bzl file) even where one does, and an exercise's or a
# unit's fixtures/ folder that no rule here covers.
**/tests/*/fixtures/** -text
